# Hotkeys and Lost-Window Recovery

Status: IN_PROGRESS
Milestone: M2 Core utility
Dependencies: 05_WINDOW_SHELL.todo

## Purpose

Keep a no-taskbar utility discoverable and controllable by its owner, even after failures.

## Tasks

- [ ] Register configurable reveal/hide and Edit/Present hotkeys with explicit conflict handling.
- [x] Use RegisterHotKey, not a keystroke hook; avoid reserved F12/Windows-key bindings.
- [ ] Establish a working recovery path before hiding the only visible controls.
- [x] Implement current-user single-instance ownership and current-user-only Reveal IPC.
- [x] Reject unknown recovery commands and bound pipe connect/read timeouts.
- [x] Recenter to the primary work area and recheck capture setup on second-launch recovery.
- [ ] Unregister/re-register safely; preserve a working route when rebinding fails.
- [ ] If the owner enables a tray option, test Explorer restart and document its visible shell presence.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Hotkey conflict, missing monitor, invisible startup, and duplicate launch never permanently strand users.
- [ ] Recovery requires no Task Manager, taskbar button, administrator rights, or global input recording.
- [ ] Reveal cannot bypass the capture gate.
- [ ] Hide/reveal meets approved latency budgets; exit really terminates rather than merely hides.
- [ ] Single-instance signaling accepts only bounded same-user recovery operations.

## Completion evidence

- Fixed Ctrl+Alt+Space/ Ctrl+Alt+E and per-user named-pipe recovery implemented.
- Configurable bindings, exhaustive startup/stale-ownership/monitor scenarios and owner acceptance remain open.

## Completion log

- 2026-10-07 - Planned; recoverability is core behavior, not optional polish.
